      "claimId": "MLS-CLM-4FADFF31B1AFE39969D3AAC8",
      "sectionKey": "in-brief",
      "summary": "Korean prenominal forms of verbs/adjectives modify a following noun; the ending varies by word class and tense.",
      "claimType": "general",
      "materiality": "substantial"
    },
    {
      "claimId": "MLS-CLM-4D51760C6CEE5902D97F70B4",
      "sectionKey": "adjectives",
      "summary": "For descriptive adjectives, -(으)ㄴ is used for present modification with the described vowel/ㄹ versus consonant allomorphy; examples include 맑은 날씨, 따뜻한 날씨, 예쁜 꽃.",
      "claimType": "general",
      "materiality": "substantial"
    },
    {
      "claimId": "MLS-CLM-CE17C4E211F747BC8E45F1EE",
      "sectionKey": "verbs",
      "summary": "For action verbs, -(으)ㄴ is used in the taught past prenominal pattern, as in 노래를 한 사람, 춤을 춘 사람, 선물을 받은 사람.",
      "claimType": "general",
      "materiality": "substantial"
    },
    {
      "claimId": "MLS-CLM-40D26A5BF473E2608D390D60",
      "sectionKey": "scope",
      "summary": "The NIKL says relative versus appositive adnominal clauses are generally distinguished by whether the modified noun can be interpreted as a clause constituent; this entry does not elaborate the analysis.",
      "claimType": "general",
      "materiality": "substantial"
    }
  ],
